@node	4.2, 4.3, 4.1, Chapter 4
@section Variations on the Guest Language --- Lazy Evaluation

Now that we have an evaluator expressed as a TypeScript program, we can
experiment
with alternative choices in language design simply by modifying the evaluator.
Indeed, new languages are often invented by first writing an evaluator that
embeds the new language within an existing high-level language.  For example,
if we wish to discuss some aspect of a proposed modification to the guest
language
with another member of the language community, we can supply an evaluator that embodies
the change.  The recipient can then experiment with the new evaluator and send
back comments as further modifications.  Not only does the high-level
implementation base make it easier to test and debug the evaluator; in
addition, the embedding enables the designer to snarf@footnote{Snarf: ``To
grab, especially a large document or file for the purpose of using it either
with or without the owner's permission.''  Snarf down: ``To snarf, sometimes
with the connotation of absorbing, processing, or understanding.''  (These
definitions were snarfed from @ref{Steele et al. 1983}.  See also @ref{Raymond 1993}.)}
features from the underlying language, just as our embedded evaluator uses
primitives and control structure from the underlying TypeScript host.  Only
later (if ever) need the designer go to the trouble of building a complete
implementation in a low-level language or in hardware.  In this section and the
next we explore some variations on the guest language that provide significant
additional
expressive power.

These variations are @newterm{named experiments}, not properties of the core
language and not properties of native TypeScript.  The lazy variants run only
under the modes @code{lazy-memoized-experiment} and
@code{lazy-recompute-experiment}, which admit the explicit @code{delay} and
@code{force} extension nodes and reject everything else outside the core
grammar.  The default evaluator and the native oracle remain eager, argument
values are computed once left-to-right, and no experimental program is ever
presented to the native runtime as if JavaScript itself were lazy.  Each
experiment has its own finite reference model and its own behavioral oracle;
the core checker refuses the extension nodes outright.

