@subsection An Interpreter with Lazy Evaluation

In this section we will implement a normal-order language that is the same as
the guest language except that closures are non-strict in each argument.
Primitive procedures will still be strict.  It is not difficult to modify the
evaluator of @ref{4.1.1} so that the language it interprets behaves
this way.  Almost all the required changes center around procedure application.

The lazy evaluator of this section lives beside the metacircular one, in
@code{02-lazy.ts}, and is reached through the named experiments
@code{runLazySource(text, mode)} for mode @code{lazy-memoized-experiment} or
@code{lazy-recompute-experiment}: it reuses the 4.1 syntax layer, environment
operations,
and primitive table wholesale, and changes only the cases the section
changes.  Because both evaluators share one data representation, the exercises
can build their variants from the same pieces, and each variant differs from
the section's evaluator by a named knob rather than by a forked dispatch.

The basic idea is that, when applying a procedure, the interpreter must
determine which arguments are to be evaluated and which are to be delayed.  The
delayed arguments are not evaluated; instead, they are transformed into objects
called @newterm{thunks}.@footnote{The word @newterm{thunk} was invented by an
informal working group that was discussing the implementation of call-by-name
in Algol 60.  They observed that most of the analysis of (``thinking about'')
the expression could be done at compile time; thus, at run time, the expression
would already have been ``thunk'' about (@ref{Ingerman et al. 1960}).} The thunk must
contain the information required to produce the value of the argument when it
is needed, as if it had been evaluated at the time of the application.  Thus,
the thunk must contain the argument expression and the environment in which the
procedure application is being evaluated.

The process of evaluating the expression in a thunk is called
@newterm{forcing}.@footnote{This is analogous to the use of @code{force} on the
delayed objects that were introduced in @ref{Chapter 3} to represent streams.
The critical difference between what we are doing here and what we did in
@ref{Chapter 3} is that we are building delaying and forcing into the
evaluator, and thus making this uniform and automatic throughout the language.}
In general, a thunk will be forced only when its value is needed: when it is
passed to a primitive procedure that will use the value of the thunk; when it
is the value of a predicate of a conditional; and when it is the value of an
operator that is about to be applied as a procedure.  One design choice we have
available is whether or not to @newterm{memoize} thunks, as we did with delayed
objects in @ref{3.5.1}.  With memoization, the first time a thunk is
forced, it stores the value that is computed.  Subsequent forcings simply
return the stored value without repeating the computation.  We'll make our
interpreter memoize, because this is more efficient for many applications.
There are tricky considerations here, however.@footnote{Lazy evaluation
combined with memoization is sometimes referred to as @newterm{call-by-need}
argument passing, in contrast to @newterm{call-by-name} argument passing.
(Call-by-name, introduced in Algol 60, is similar to non-memoized lazy
evaluation.)  As language designers, we can build our evaluator to memoize, not
to memoize, or leave this an option for programmers (@ref{Exercise 4.31}).  As
you might expect from @ref{Chapter 3}, these choices raise issues that become
both subtle and confusing in the presence of assignments.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.27}
and @ref{Exercise 4.29}.)  An excellent article by @ref{Clinger (1982)}
attempts to clarify the multiple dimensions of confusion that arise here.}

@subsubheading Modifying the evaluator

The main difference between the lazy evaluator and the one in @ref{4.1}
is in the handling of procedure applications in @code{evaluate} and
@code{applyProcedure}.

The application case of @code{evaluate} becomes

@example typescript
case "call": @{
  const procedure = actualValue(expr.callee, env);
  if (procedure.tag !== "ok") @{
    return procedure;
  @}
  return applyProcedure(procedure.value, expr.args, env);
@}
@end example

@noindent
This is almost the same as the application case of @code{evaluate} in
@ref{4.1.1}.  For lazy evaluation, however, we call @code{applyProcedure} with
the argument expressions, rather than the values produced by evaluating them.
Since we will need the environment to construct thunks if the arguments are to
be delayed, we must pass this as well.  We still evaluate the callee, because
@code{applyProcedure} needs the actual procedure to be applied in order to
dispatch on its type (primitive versus closure) and apply it.

Whenever we need the actual value of an expression, we use

@example typescript
export const actualValue = (
  expr: Expr,
  env: Env,
  memoize = true
): Outcome => @{
  const value = evaluate(expr, env);
  return value.tag === "ok" ? forceIt(value.value, memoize) : value;
@};
@end example

@noindent
instead of just @code{evaluate}, so that if the expression's value is a thunk, it
will be forced.

Our new version of @code{applyProcedure} is also almost the same as the version in
@ref{4.1.1}.  The difference is that @code{evaluate} has passed in
unevaluated argument expressions: For primitive procedures (which are strict),
we evaluate all the arguments before applying the primitive; for closures
(which are non-strict) we delay all the arguments before applying
the procedure.

@example typescript
export const applyProcedure = (
  procedure: Value,
  argumentExps: ReadonlyArray<Arg>,
  env: Env,
): Outcome => @{
  if (isPrimitive(procedure)) @{
    // changed: the operands force here
    const args = listOfArgValues(argumentExps, env);
    return args.tag === "ok"
      ? applyPrimitiveProcedure(procedure, args.value)
      : args;
  @}
  if (isClosure(procedure)) @{
    // changed: the operands delay instead of evaluating
    const args = listOfDelayedArgs(procedure, argumentExps, env);
    if (args.tag !== "ok") @{
      return args;
    @}
    const frame = extendEnvironment(procedure, args.value);
    return frame.tag === "ok"
      ? executeProgram(
        evalSequence(procedure.body.body, frame.value))
      : frame;
  @}
  return fail(@{ tag: "not-callable", detail: format(procedure) @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
The procedures that process the arguments are just like @code{evalArgs}
from @ref{4.1.1}, except that @code{listOfDelayedArgs} delays the
arguments instead of evaluating them, and @code{listOfArgValues} uses
@code{actualValue} instead of @code{evaluate}:

@example typescript
const listOfArgValues = (
  exps: ReadonlyArray<Arg>,
  env: Env
): Outcome => @{
  const args: Value[] = [];
  for (const arg of exps) @{
    const value = actualValue(arg.expr, env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return value;
    @}
    args.push(value.value);
  @}
  return ok(args);
@};

const listOfDelayedArgs = (
  procedure: Closure,
  exps: ReadonlyArray<Arg>,
  env: Env,
): Outcome => @{
  // binds each operand per its mode: computed, recomputing, or strict
  const args: Value[] = [];
  let position = 0;
  for (const arg of exps) @{
    const bound = bindOperand(procedure, arg.expr, position, env);
    if (bound.tag !== "ok") @{
      return bound;
    @}
    args.push(bound.value);
    position = position + 1;
  @}
  return ok(args);
@};
@end example

@noindent
The other place we must change the evaluator is in the handling of @code{if},
where we must use @code{actualValue} instead of @code{evaluate} to get the value
of the test expression before testing whether it is true or false:

@example typescript
const evalIf = (stmt: Stmt, env: Env): Completion => @{
  const predicate = actualValue(stmt.test, env);
  if (predicate.tag !== "ok") @{
    return @{ tag: "error", error: predicate.error @};
  @}
  if (typeof predicate.value !== "boolean") @{
    return @{
      tag: "error",
      error: @{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: "if",
        detail: "test is not a boolean" @},
    @};
  @}
  return predicate.value
    ? execBody(stmt.consequent, env)
    : stmt.alternative === null
      ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
      : execBody(stmt.alternative, env);
@};
@end example

@noindent
Finally, we must change the @code{driverLoop} procedure (@ref{4.1.4})
to use @code{actualValue} instead of @code{evaluate}, so that if a delayed value
is propagated back to the read-eval-print loop, it will be forced before being
printed.  We also change the prompts to indicate that this is the lazy
evaluator:

@example typescript
// The book's inputPrompt and outputPrompt are the literal lines
// the transcript reports for every input: ";;; L-Eval input:" and
// ";;; L-Eval value:".

export const lazyDriverLoop = (
  env: Env,
  inputs: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> => @{
  const transcript: string[] = [];
  for (const input of inputs) @{
    transcript.push(";;; L-Eval input:", input);
    const admitted = admitSource(input, "lazy-memoized-experiment");
    if (!admitted.ok) @{
      transcript.push(
        ";;; L-Eval value:",
        formatDiagnostic(admitted.diagnostics)
      );
      continue;
    @}
    const result = runLazySource(input, "lazy-memoized-experiment");
    transcript.push(
      ";;; L-Eval value:",
      result.outcome.tag === "ok"
        ? format(result.outcome.value)
        : format(result.outcome.error),
    );
  @}
  return transcript;
@};
@end example

@noindent
With these changes made, we can start the evaluator and test it.  The
successful evaluation of the @code{tryIt} call discussed in
@ref{4.2.1} indicates that the interpreter is performing lazy evaluation:

@example typescript
const session = runLazySource(`
  const tryIt = (a: number, b: number): number => (a === 0 ? 1 : b);
  tryIt(0, missing);
`, "lazy-memoized-experiment");
// session.outcome is ok with the value 1
@end example

@subsubheading Representing thunks

Our evaluator must arrange to create thunks when procedures are applied to
arguments and to force these thunks later.  A thunk must package an expression
together with the environment, so that the argument can be produced later.  To
force the thunk, we simply extract the expression and environment from the
thunk and evaluate the expression in the environment.  We use
@code{actualValue} rather than @code{evaluate} so that in case the value of the
expression is itself a thunk, we will force that, and so on, until we reach
something that is not a thunk.  The book builds this as a procedure over the
thunk representation; the edition's forcing machinery is the @code{forceIt} of
the lazy evaluator, shown in @ref{4.2.2}'s module as

@example typescript
const forceIt = (value: Value, memoize = true): Outcome => @{
  if (!isThunk(value)) @{
    return ok(value);
  @}
  if (memoize && value.evaluated) @{
    return ok(value.computed);
  @}
  const result = evaluate(value.expr, value.env);
  if (result.tag !== "ok") @{
    return result;
  @}
  const nested = forceIt(result.value, memoize);
  if (nested.tag !== "ok") @{
    return nested;
  @}
  if (memoize) @{
    value.computed = nested.value;
    value.evaluated = true;
  @}
  return ok(nested.value);
@};
@end example

@noindent
One easy way to package an expression with an environment is to make a record
containing the expression and the environment.  Thus, we create a thunk as
follows (the @code{ThunkValue} member of the value union of @ref{4.1.3}):

@example typescript
type ThunkValue = @{
  tag: "thunk";
  expr: Expr;
  env: Env;
  computed: Value | undefined;
  evaluated: boolean;
@};

const delayIt = (expr: Expr, env: Env): ThunkValue => (@{
  tag: "thunk",
  expr: expr,
  env: env,
  computed: undefined,
  evaluated: false,
@});

export const isThunk = (value: Value): value is ThunkValue =>
  value !== null && typeof value === "object" && value.tag === "thunk";

const exprOf = (thunk: ThunkValue): Expr => thunk.expr;

const envOf = (thunk: ThunkValue): Env => thunk.env;
@end example

@noindent
Actually, what we want for our interpreter is not quite this, but rather thunks
that have been computed.  When a thunk is forced, we will turn it into an
evaluated thunk by replacing the stored expression with its value and marking
it as already evaluated.@footnote{Notice that we also erase the
@code{environment} from the thunk
once the expression's value has been computed.  This makes no difference in the
values returned by the interpreter.  It does help save space, however, because
removing the reference from the thunk to the @code{environment} once it is no longer
needed allows this structure to be @newterm{garbage-collected} and its space
recycled, as we will discuss in @ref{5.3}.

Similarly, we could have allowed unneeded environments in the computed delayed
objects of @ref{3.5.1} to be @newterm{garbage-collected}, by having
the memoizing cell drop its reference to the stored
procedure (which includes the environment in which the delay
was evaluated) after storing its value.}  The
@code{evaluated} flag of @code{ThunkValue} and the write of @code{computed} above
are the book's mutation of a thunk into an evaluated thunk: the tagged thunk
list becomes the fields of one record, and the
following selectors read it back.  In the @code{lazy-recompute-experiment}
mode @code{memoize} is false, so the evaluated flag never latches, so every forcing recomputes.

@example typescript
// The book's isEvaluatedThunk: the evaluated flag of the record.
// The book's thunkValue: the computed slot, which holds the stored
// value.

const evaluatedThunkValue = (thunk: ThunkValue): Value | undefined =>
  thunk.evaluated ? thunk.computed : undefined;
@end example

@noindent
Notice that the same @code{delayIt} procedure works both with and without
memoization; the @code{memoize} argument decides whether the first forcing
stores its value, and exercise @ref{Exercise 4.31} drives it per parameter.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.27}Exercise 4.27:} Suppose we type in the following
definitions to the lazy evaluator:

@example typescript
