@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.30}Exercise 4.30:} Cy D. Fect, a reformed C
programmer, is worried that some side effects may never take place, because the
lazy evaluator doesn't force the expressions in a sequence.  Since the value of
an expression in a sequence other than the last one is not used (the expression
is there only for its effect, such as assigning to a variable or printing),
there can be no subsequent use of this value (e.g., as an argument to a
primitive procedure) that will cause it to be forced.  Cy thus thinks that when
evaluating sequences, we must force all expressions in the sequence except the
final one.  He proposes to modify @code{evalSequence} from @ref{4.1.1}
to use @code{actualValue} rather than @code{evaluate}:

@example typescript
// Cy's proposal: the non-final statements force.
const evalSequence = (
  statements: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
  env: Env,
): Completion => @{
  let result: Completion = @{
    tag: "normal",
    value: undefined,
  @};
  for (const statement of statements) @{
    const isLast = statements.indexOf(
      statement) === statements.length - 1;
    result = isLast
      ? execBody(statement, env)
      : forceStatement(statement, env);
    if (result.tag !== "normal") @{
      return result;
    @}
  @}
  return result;
@};
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
Ben Bitdiddle thinks Cy is wrong.  He shows Cy the @code{forEach} procedure
described in @ref{Exercise 2.23}, which gives an important example of a
sequence with side effects:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  const forEach = (
    proc: (x: number) => void,
    items: number[]
  ): string => @{
    if (items.length === 0) @{
      return "done";
    @}
    const first = items[0];
    if (first !== undefined) @{
      proc(first);
    @}
    forEach(proc, items.slice(1));
    return "done";
  @};
`)
@end example

He claims that the evaluator in the text (with the original
@code{evalSequence}) handles this correctly:

@example typescript
const transcript = lazyDriverLoop(theGlobalEnvironment, [
  `
  const forEach = (
    proc: (x: number) => void,
    items: number[]
  ): string => @{ @dots{} @};
  `,
  "forEach((x) => reportLine(x), [57, 321, 88]);",
]);
// 57
// 321
// 88

// ;;; L-Eval value:
// done
@end example

where @code{reportLine} prints its argument on its own transcript line.
Explain why Ben is right about the behavior of @code{forEach}.

@item
Cy agrees that Ben is right about the @code{forEach} example, but says that
that's not the kind of program he was thinking about when he proposed his
change to @code{evalSequence}.  He defines the following two procedures in the
lazy evaluator:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  const p1 = (x: LazyList): LazyList => @{
    const y = pair(x, twoList);
    return y;
  @};

  const p2 = (x: LazyList): LazyList => @{
    const p = (e: LazyList): LazyList => x;
    return p(pair(x, twoList));
  @};
`)
@end example

What are the values of @code{p1(1)} and @code{p2(1)} with the original
@code{evalSequence}?  What would the values be with Cy's proposed change to
@code{evalSequence}?

@item
Cy also points out that changing @code{evalSequence} as he proposes does not
affect the behavior of the example in part a.  Explain why this is true.

@item
How do you think sequences ought to be treated in the lazy evaluator?  Do you
like Cy's approach, the approach in the text, or some other approach?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.31}Exercise 4.31:} The approach taken in this
section is somewhat unpleasant, because it makes an incompatible change to
the guest language.  It might be nicer to implement lazy evaluation as an
@newterm{upward-compatible extension}, that is, so that ordinary core
programs will work as before.  We can do this by extending the syntax of
procedure declarations to let the user control whether or not arguments are to
be delayed.  While we're at it, we may as well also give the user the choice
between delaying with and without memoization.  For example, the definition

@example typescript
readAll(`
  const f = (
    a: number,
    b: ⟨@var{lazy}⟩ number,
    c: number,
    d: ⟨@var{lazy-memo}⟩ number,
  ): number => @{
    @r{…}
  @};
`)
@end example

@noindent
would define @code{f} to be a procedure of four arguments, where the first and
third arguments are evaluated when the procedure is called, the second argument
is delayed, and the fourth argument is both delayed and computed.  Thus,
ordinary procedure definitions will produce the same behavior as ordinary core
mode,
while adding the @code{lazy-memo} declaration to each parameter of
every closure will produce the behavior of the lazy evaluator
defined in this section. Design and implement the changes required to produce
such an extension to the guest language.  You will have to implement new syntax
procedures
to handle the new syntax for declarations.  You must also arrange for
@code{evaluate} or @code{applyProcedure} to determine when arguments are to be
delayed, and
to force or delay arguments accordingly, and you must arrange for forcing to
memoize or not, as appropriate.
@end quotation

@node	4.2.3, 4.3, 4.1, 4.2
@subsection Streams as Lazy Lists

In @ref{3.5.1}, we showed how to implement streams as delayed lists.
We introduced explicit delay operations, which allowed
us to construct a ``promise'' to compute the tail of a stream, without
actually fulfilling that promise until later.  We could use this general
technique of introducing special forms whenever we need more control over the
evaluation process, but this is awkward.  For one thing, a special form is not
a first-class object like a procedure, so we cannot use it together with
higher-order procedures.@footnote{This is precisely the issue with the
@code{unless} procedure, as in @ref{Exercise 4.26}.}  Additionally, we were
forced to create streams as a new kind of data object similar but not identical
to lists, and this required us to reimplement many ordinary list operations
(@code{mapList}, @code{append}, and so on) for use with streams.

With lazy evaluation, streams and lists can be identical, so there is no need
for special forms or for separate list and stream operations.  All we need to
do is to arrange matters so that the pair constructor is non-strict.  One way to
accomplish this is to extend the lazy evaluator to allow for non-strict
primitives, and to implement the pair constructor as one of these.  An easier
way is to
recall (@ref{2.1.3}) that there is no fundamental need to implement
a pair as a primitive at all.  Instead, we can represent pairs as
procedures:@footnote{This is the procedural representation described in
@ref{Exercise 2.4}.  Essentially any procedural representation (e.g., a
message-passing implementation) would do as well.  Notice that we can install
these definitions in the lazy evaluator simply by typing them at the driver
loop.  If we had originally included the pair constructor and its selectors as
primitives in the global environment, they will be redefined.  (Also see
@ref{Exercise 4.33} and @ref{Exercise 4.34}.)}

@example typescript
const pairDefinitions = readAll(`
  interface LazyList @{
    select: (m: (head: number, tail: LazyList) => number) => number;
  @}

  const pair = (head: number, tail: LazyList): LazyList => (@{
    select: (m) => m(head, tail),
  @});

  const head = (items: LazyList): number => items.select((h, t) => h);

  const tail = (items: LazyList): LazyList => items.select((h, t) => t);
`);
@end example

@noindent
In terms of these basic operations, the standard definitions of the list
operations will work with infinite lists (streams) as well as finite ones, and
the stream operations can be implemented as list operations.  Here are some
examples (the self-referential definitions become self-referential
@emph{functions}, because the guest checker rejects a name used in its own
initializer; the delayed argument makes each recursive call safe):

@example typescript
const listDefinitions = readAll(`
  const listRef = (items: LazyList, n: number): number =>
    n === 0 ? head(items) : listRef(tail(items), n - 1);

  const mapList = (
    transform: (x: number) => number,
    items: LazyList
  ): LazyList =>
    pair(transform(head(items)), mapList(transform, tail(items)));

  const scaleList = (items: LazyList, factor: number): LazyList =>
    mapList((x) => x * factor, items);

  const addLists = (list1: LazyList, list2: LazyList): LazyList =>
    pair(head(list1) + head(list2), addLists(tail(list1), tail(list2)));

  const ones = (): LazyList => pair(1, ones());

  const integers = (): LazyList =>
    pair(1, addLists(ones(), integers()));
`);

const session = runLazySource(
  listDefinitions + "\nlistRef(integers(), 17);",
  "lazy-memoized-experiment",
);
// session.outcome is ok with the value 18
@end example

@noindent
Note that these lazy lists are even lazier than the streams of @ref{Chapter 3}:
The head of the list, as well as the tail, is
delayed.@footnote{This permits us to create delayed versions of more general
kinds of list structures, not just sequences.  @ref{Hughes 1990} discusses some
applications of ``lazy trees.''}  In fact, even accessing the head or
tail of a lazy pair need not force the value of a list element.  The
value will be forced only when it is really needed, e.g., for use as the
argument of a primitive, or to be printed as an answer.

Lazy pairs also help with the problem that arose with streams in
@ref{3.5.4}, where we found that formulating stream models of systems with
loops may require us to sprinkle our programs with explicit delay
operations.  With lazy
evaluation, all arguments to procedures are delayed uniformly.  For instance,
we can implement procedures to integrate lists and solve differential equations
as we originally intended in @ref{3.5.4}:

@example typescript
const solveDefinitions = readAll(`
  const integral = (
    integrand: LazyList,
    initialValue: number,
    dt: number,
): LazyList => pair(
    initialValue,
    addLists(
      scaleList(integrand, dt),
      integral(integrand, initialValue, dt)));

  const solve = (
    f: (y: number) => number,
    y0: number,
    dt: number
  ): LazyList => @{
    const y = mapList(f, integral(solve(f, y0, dt), y0, dt));
    return y;
  @};
`);

const session = runLazySource(
  solveDefinitions + "\nlistRef(solve((x) => x, 1, 0.001), 1000);",
  "lazy-memoized-experiment",
);
// session.outcome is ok with a value near 2.716924
@end example

